{"When evt.destinationCalendar is null or an empty array, destructuring yields undefined for the first element and accessing .integration on it will throw at runtime": 0.5133668780326843, "The newly added externalId parameter is only echoed back in the return value and never forwarded to the underlying calendar.createEvent call, so the calendar provider will still use its default destination calendar instead of the intended external calendar, breaking multi-calendar/multi-host routing": 0.3087989389896393, "The main host calendar used to derive the organizer email is always taken as the first element of the destination calendars array, ignoring which credential is actually being used, so in multi-host scenarios the event organizer email can point to the wrong host's calendar": 0.9163209795951843, "For updates, the main host calendar used to decide the organizer email is always the first destination calendar and does not respect which external calendar ID is being updated, so the organizer email can mismatch the calendar actually being updated": 0.9577017426490784, "In the recurring booking cancellation path, the code iterates over bookingToDelete.user.credentials and calls deleteEvent for all of the user's calendar credentials, ignoring the specific calendarCredential associated with each booking reference, which can leave some host calendar events uncleared in multi-host scenarios": 0.6277600526809692, "The catch block in loadUsers rethrows PrismaClientKnownRequestError.message directly to the client with a 400 status, leaking internal database error details": 0.7141759991645813, "When the event type and organizer have no destination calendar but some additional collective hosts do, evt.destinationCalendar is null and the optional chaining on .push silently drops all teamDestinationCalendars, so those hosts never get their calendars included": 0.9696667194366455}